Eosinophilic Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (Mild Prodromal)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Peripheral eosinophilia, recurrent nasal polyps, pre-vasculitic phase

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-016
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of nocturnal wheeze and dyspnea requiring exclusion of severe acute asthma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, unresolving nocturnal dyspnea and wheeze in a patient with adult-onset allergic rhinitis and mild asthma phenotype. Immediate assessment was strictly necessary to rule out life-threatening bronchospasm or impending respiratory failure prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't catch my breath, my chest is tight, and my inhaler isn't doing anything. I felt like I was suffocating when I tried to lie down.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports adult-onset allergic rhinitis and mild asthma phenotype, managed typically with intermittent inhalers. Tonight, the patient developed a steady increase in chest tightness and expiratory wheeze commencing at 20:00. By 23:00, the patient was unable to lie flat without coughing spasms and orthopnea. Denies fever, purulent sputum, or acute chest pain. The failure of the rescue inhaler precipitated severe anxiety.
Functional Status: Independent, currently sitting upright in the tripod position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, high anxiety regarding respiratory distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, speaking in full sentences but visibly anxious, mild tachypnea.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals widespread polyphonic expiratory wheeze bilaterally with good air entry. No silent chest, no accessory muscle use. Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia, likely secondary to salbutamol use and anxiety. ENT exam shows boggy nasal turbinates consistent with recurrent nasal polyps. No rash or peripheral neuropathy noted. Peripheral eosinophilia noted on recent routine labs (pre-vasculitic phase).
Environmental Observations: Visible dust in the bedroom, poor ventilation, pets present.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Using PRN salbutamol.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Asthma Exacerbation in the setting of Eosinophilic Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (Mild Prodromal) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of bilateral basal crackles, normal jugular venous pressure, and lack of peripheral edema.

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominent wheeze pointing to airway disease, no pleuritic chest pain, and SpO2 maintained at 96% on room air.

Infective Exacerbation of Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of purulent sputum, and lack of localized crepitations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (living alone with acute respiratory distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute nocturnal bronchospasm refractory to initial rescue therapy, raising a high clinical suspicion for impending respiratory failure. Triage correctly prioritized a physical exam to exclude a silent chest or severe systemic vasculitic manifestation. Detailed auscultation confirmed moderate, reversible bronchospasm without signs of respiratory exhaustion or imminent collapse. Immediate administration of oral corticosteroids and supervised bronchodilation stabilized the patient, preventing an unnecessary emergency department admission and ensuring respiratory safety that could not be deferred until the morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered single dose of oral prednisolone 50mg and supervised administration of salbutamol via spacer (4 puffs). Reassessed after 20 minutes with marked improvement in wheeze and RR dropping to 16.
Positive Behavioral Change: Sit upright in a chair and take slow, deep breaths through your nose while using your spacer exactly as demonstrated. By utilizing the spacer correctly, you ensure the medication reaches the deep airways, which will rapidly stop the coughing spasms and allow you to sleep comfortably.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to discuss maintenance inhaler escalation and autoimmune blood panels.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if your lips turn blue, if you become too breathless to speak in full sentences, or if your reliever medication completely stops working.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal rheumatological and respiratory workup; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This type of adult-onset asthma is driven by specific inflammatory cells (eosinophils) and typically requires daily preventative steroid inhalers to remain stable.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you feel confused, drowsy, or if the severe chest tightness returns within the next two hours despite resting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The airway tubes are temporarily narrowed by inflammation and muscle spasm. The steroid pill reduces the swelling, and the inhaler relaxes the muscles.
Actionable Steps: Keep your reliever inhaler beside your bed. Sleep propped up on two pillows tonight and completely avoid the dusty areas of the house.

